# copyWith nullable fields

For a nullable field, a plain `T?` argument cannot tell "omitted" from "set to null". Use `CWValue<T>` from `package:core/core.dart`. `T` is the non-null field type (`CWValue<String>`, not `CWValue<String?>`).

Do not declare a local `CWValue`. Resolve with `CWValue.resolve`. Non-nullable fields stay `T?` with `?? this.field`.

```dart
import 'package:core/core.dart';

class Post {
  final String title;
  final DateTime? updatedAt;

  const Post({required this.title, this.updatedAt});

  // ❌ BAD — copyWith(updatedAt: null) keeps the old value
  Post copyWith({String? title, DateTime? updatedAt}) => Post(
    title: title ?? this.title,
    updatedAt: updatedAt ?? this.updatedAt,
  );

  // ✅ GOOD
  Post copyWith({String? title, CWValue<DateTime>? updatedAt}) => Post(
    title: title ?? this.title,
    updatedAt: CWValue.resolve(updatedAt, this.updatedAt),
  );
}

post.copyWith(title: 'Next');
post.copyWith(updatedAt: CWValue(time));
post.copyWith(updatedAt: const CWValue(null));
```

Notes:

- Omit the `CWValue` argument to keep the current value. Pass `CWValue(null)` to clear it.
- Do not wrap non-nullable fields.
- Leave generated `copyWith` methods alone (json_serializable, Freezed, Flutter `TextStyle.copyWith`).
